Z||000001||Carrolles||10-05-2000||08:23 AM||||I'd suggest getting the PW from a donor car.  You'll need the lift motors, switches, cross car harness, in-door harnesses, and a 30amp circuit breaker for the fuse box.<BR>I picked up a set of pw/pdl doors at Ed Parks swap meet last year for $45.00.  I got the wiring from the junk yard for $15.00.<P>Look at the Fiero Online Service Guide<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Garage/5007/fiero_osg/mod.add.PW.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Garage/5007/fiero_osg/mod.add.PW.html</A> <P>for links to instructions.  I believe Michigan Fieros also has links.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.tir.com/~jaski/pw_pl_install.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.tir.com/~jaski/pw_pl_install.htm</A> <P>I'm in the process of installing pw/pdl from a donor in my 85SE along with an aftermarket cruise control (at least I was until I got a project 85GT to work on for my wife).<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||10-05-2000||09:33 AM||||I'm just about done adding power windows. I'll be updating the cave article about it soon. in the mean time.<P>Time<P>expect to take a day to remove the parts from the old car.<P>A day to install the harness and clean up whatever else needs doing under the dash. (You want to put the wires in the OE spot for this. HEavy wires that carry lots of current. You don't want to chance them shorting.)<P>A 5-6 hours per door. I did mine by swapping the whole door and putting my exterior skins on them. You'll likey need to adjust the window tracks and stops. you want to clean and lube everything.<P>I'm not quite finished yet. I have to make a patern and cut some new barrier sheets for the doors. The paper ones tend to self distruct while you work. (If the scum sheets aren't replaced you can get water on the back of the inner panel which wil start mold growing on them. not good.)<P>Total I'd say there's 20-30 hours of work in doing a good job on it.<P>4-6 hours pulling parts.<BR>6-8 hours intalling all the dash & consol stuff and cleaning up a set of switches.<BR>5-6 hours per door.<BR>a few hours doing detail b.s. planing the whole mess<BR>Add even more time if you need to paint up a set of mirror shells.<P>The best order of install is...<BR>do all the dash & console stuff<BR>do the passengers door.<BR>do the drivers door.<P>That way if you can't do it all in one shot you end up with a car that's drivable in the most convienent state for each stage of the project. (I drove mine for weeks with just the dash done. then about 2 weeks with just the passenger door working before I got the other door on.)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-05-2000).]||||reg||1
